## Migration Analysis

Your code has **4 thread-abort patterns** that need migration:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| Cancellable work loop | `while (_running)` | `CancellationToken` |
| `ThreadAbortException` catch | `catch` block | `OperationCanceledException` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | catch body | `token.IsCancellationRequested` check |
| `Thread.Abort()` force-stop | `Stop()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;

        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token so blocking calls can unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (moved from ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just loop back and the while condition exits
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signals cooperative cancellation

        // Wait for the thread to finish (replaces Sleep + Abort)
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

## Key Changes to Dependent Methods

If `GetNextItem()` blocks (e.g., on a queue), it **must** accept the token — otherwise cancellation stalls:

```csharp
// BEFORE: may block forever, only Thread.Abort could break it
private Item GetNextItem() => _queue.Take();

// AFTER: unblocks when token is cancelled
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token) => _queue.Take(token);
```

---

## Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Not passing token to blocking calls** | `CancellationToken` can't interrupt `Thread.Sleep` or unaware blocking calls. Replace `Thread.Sleep(ms)` with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)`. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers/callbacks. The `IDisposable` pattern above handles this. |
| **Expecting instant cancellation** | Cooperative cancellation only fires at checkpoints. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive operations if needed. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | The `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter ensures you only catch *your* cancellation, not an unrelated one. |
| **`Thread.Join` without timeout** | If the worker ignores the token, `Join()` hangs forever. Always use a timeout. |

The fundamental shift: .NET Framework's `Thread.Abort` *forcibly injected* an exception. In .NET 8, your code must *voluntarily check* `CancellationToken` — cancellation only works if every blocking call in the chain cooperates.
